/* book exam traps: Tie lines are measured lines through the two equilibrium layers. A line drawn parallel to a side of the triangle is a line of constant composition of one component, not a tie line. | The mixture point lies on the straight line FS and on a tie line, never on the binodal curve itself unless the mixture is just saturated. | K and β are different: K = 1.54 here says how much solvent is needed, β = 29.4 says how cleanly A is separated from B. A solvent with large K and β near 1 is useless. | In the lever rule E/M = RM/RE, the arm opposite E is RM. Swapping the arms gives E = 72.7 kg instead of 147.3 kg. | The plait point is where the layers become identical, not the richest possible extract. */
